<https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entries/bertalanffy-1968-gst>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/atlas-core> ;
    rdfs:label "Atlas entry — Bertalanffy 1968, General System Theory"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Entry 005. The canonical book, entered after the 1972 article (entry 004)
rather than before it, because the only held copy is an OCR in which eq. (3.1) did not survive
— the equation below was transcribed from the page image and screenshot-verified on 2026-08-05.
The 1968/1972 pair makes the revision citable: what changed between them is proved in Lean
(bert68To72Pre, environment_is_new), not narrated."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Bearer — the 1968 book, distinct from entry 004's 1972 article
##############################################################################

bearer:bertalanffy-1968-gst
    a cco:ont00000253 ;   # Information Bearing Entity
    rdfs:label "Von Bertalanffy, L. — General System Theory: Foundations, Development, Applications (1968)"@en ;
    dcterms:creator "Ludwig von Bertalanffy" ;
    atlas:authoredBy author:ludwig-von-bertalanffy ;
    dcterms:date "1968"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "New York: George Braziller, 1968. Ch. 3, 'Some System Concepts in Elementary Mathematical Consideration', pp. 55–60. Page numbers are Kindle print-equivalent pagination, corroborated against the print index entry 'mathematical definition, 56-60'." .

# NOT registered in the transcription gate (BEARER_SOURCES): the vault copy of
# this book is an OCR with systematically damaged ligatures in which the
# equation does not exist at all. Registering it would have the gate verify
# this entry's verbatim against a text known to be wrong. The apparatus below
# records what was checked instead, and by whom.


##############################################################################
# Entry 005 — the differential-equation definition
##############################################################################

entry:bertalanffy-1968-eq-3-1
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Bertalanffy (1968), General System Theory, eq. (3.1)"@en ;

    atlas:statedIn bearer:bertalanffy-1968-gst ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 3, eq. (3.1), p. 56" ;

    atlas:verbatim """A system can be defined mathematically in various ways. For illustration, we choose a system of simultaneous differential equations. Denoting some measure of elements, pᵢ (i = 1, 2, … n), by Qᵢ, these, for a finite number of elements and in the simplest case, will be of the form:

dQ₁/dt = f₁(Q₁, Q₂, … Qₙ)
dQ₂/dt = f₂(Q₁, Q₂, … Qₙ)
⋯
dQₙ/dt = fₙ(Q₁, Q₂, … Qₙ)                                    (3.1)""" ;

    # Presentation spans — exact substrings of the verbatim, enforced by the build.
    # The equation is the form; the author's own framing sentence is the context.
    atlas:displayForm """dQ₁/dt = f₁(Q₁, Q₂, … Qₙ)
dQ₂/dt = f₂(Q₁, Q₂, … Qₙ)
⋯
dQₙ/dt = fₙ(Q₁, Q₂, … Qₙ)""" ;
    atlas:displayContext "For illustration, we choose a system of simultaneous differential equations." ;

    # Conservative: only what the passage itself uses. "element" as in entry 004;
    # "measure" is new (the word is his — "some measure of elements"; the
    # state-variable reading is an interpretation and stays out of the scheme);
    # "time" is invoked notationally, as the t of dQᵢ/dt — the first entry to
    # mark the census's time row, previously used by nobody.
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:element ,
                           prim:measure ,
                           prim:time ;

    atlas:admits case:bertalanffy-organism ;

    # The 1968 shape — two positions, one arrow, proved isomorphic to Klir's.
    # This is the definition that shape was built to encode; entry 004 points at
    # Bertalanffy72Shape, which carries the environment position this one lacks.
    atlas:formalisedAs "Systems/Category/ShapeBertalanffy.lean#BertalanffyShape" ;

    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-07"^^xsd:date ;

    rdfs:comment """HVP, AND THE SCOPE OF THE CHECK IS EXACTLY THIS: Shingai read the page on
2026-08-05 — the prose against this transcription, and the equation against the page image,
because no text layer in any held source contains eq. (3.1) at all. The equation here is
transcribed from that image, screenshot-verified the same day. The held Kindle text renders
subscript i as t ("pt", "Qt") — an artifact of the rendering, not the book; the subscripts
above are as printed. Page numbering is Kindle print-equivalent pagination, corroborated by
the print index's "mathematical definition, 56-60".

THE HEDGE IS PART OF THE RECORD. The verbatim opens at "A system can be defined mathematically
in various ways" rather than at the equation: 1968 offers the differential equations as one
illustration among various possible formalisations, and cutting the hedge would harden an
avowedly illustrative choice into a definition the author did not state that way. The 1972
restatement (entry 004) carries the same hedge in the other direction — a verbal definition
pointing at unspecified mathematics.

WHAT THE REVISION CHANGED, NOW CITABLE FROM BOTH ENDS. 1968: elements and their interrelation,
no environment term — the shape has two positions and one arrow, and is proved isomorphic to
Klir's. 1972: "and with the environment" — a third position and a second arrow. The difference
is machine-checked: bert68To72Pre embeds the 1968 shape in the 1972 one (bert68To72_obj_injective),
and environment_is_new proves the environment position is not in its image. This entry is the
1968 end of that pair; mapping 003 (environment-channel.md) is where the finding is stated.

WHY THE 1972 ARTICLE WAS ENTERED FIRST: transcription discipline. See entry 004's apparatus;
this entry existing at all is the "second entry from the same tradition" that apparatus
promised.""" ;

    atlas:authorCaveat "A system can be defined mathematically in various ways." .


##############################################################################
# Cases
##############################################################################

# Moved here from entry 004 (2026-08-07): the ruling's bearer is this book —
# 1968, p. 209 — not the 1972 article. It was parked on 004 while 004 was the
# only Bertalanffy entry; now that its own bearer is in the catalogue, the case
# lives with it. The entry it is admitted by changes accordingly.

case:bertalanffy-organism
    a atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:label "an organism"@en ;
    atlas:gloss "an organism" ;
    atlas:verbatim "Any organism is a system, that is, a dynamic order of parts and processes standing in mutual interaction" ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "General System Theory (1968), p. 209, quoting Bertalanffy 1949a p. 11" ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-05"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """SAME BEARER AS THE ENTRY, since 2026-08-07. This ruling was first recorded
on entry 004 (the 1972 article) with a DIFFERENT-BEARER caveat, because the catalogue then held
no entry from the 1968 book. It moves here now that it does.

MDHC, AND THE SCOPE OF THE CHECK IS EXACTLY THIS: a human located "Any organism is a system"
on p. 209 of the book on 2026-08-05, so the page and the ruling itself are confirmed. The
remainder of the sentence — "that is, a dynamic order of parts and processes standing in
mutual interaction" — is still as the OCR gives it and has not been read word for word. HVP
needs that last step.

The page number comes from a Kindle edition reporting print-equivalent pagination; the same
edition's p. 56 for eq. (3.1) matches the print index's "mathematical definition, 56-60",
which is the corroboration for trusting its numbering here.

NOT ENCODED AS A REFUSAL: the heap of bricks. Bertalanffy calls heaps the case where a complex
"can be built up, step by step, by putting together the first separate elements" — merely
summative, as against the constitutive characteristics of a Gestalt (1968, ch. 3). That is
architecturally Klir's book-collection and Bunge's ordered-events exclusion, but he never
writes the verdict sentence, and encoding an implication as a ruling would put words in his
mouth. It belongs to the mapping layer.""" .


##############################################################################
# Stance (P3, minted 2026-08-07). One axis only, deliberately: the observer
# cell is EMPTY. Two full-text hunts found no passage in which Bertalanffy
# says what drawing the boundary does, and filling the cell by inference from
# the ontic column is the mistake P3 exists to prevent.
##############################################################################

@prefix stance: <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/stance/> .

entry:bertalanffy-1968-eq-3-1 atlas:hasStance stance:bertalanffy-1968-ontic .

stance:bertalanffy-1968-ontic
    a atlas:StanceAssertion ;
    atlas:stanceValue atlas:perspectivist ;
    atlas:verbatim "As for the connections of these stimuli, i.e., the categories of experience, they need not mirror the nexus of real events but must, with a certain tolerance allowed, be isomorphic to it" ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 10, 'The Perspectivistic View'" ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-drafted from P3-stance-axes.md)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-07"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """His own word — the chapter title names the position — which is why the
value is "perspectivist" rather than a characterisation in our words. The constraint is argued
evolutionarily, not asserted: categories that failed to correspond would have been selected
out, so the perspective is not free. The stance verbatim's "isomorphic ... with a certain
tolerance" applies to knowledge the same relation the shape categories apply to
formalisations; whether that is a connection or a pun is a mapping question, not asserted
here.

Attached to THIS entry, not 004: the passage is in the 1968 book, this entry's bearer. P3
read the chapter on 2026-08-03 (gst-1968-full-text.md:11478–11585). MDHC as of 2026-08-08:
model-drafted 2026-08-07, checked by Shingai the next day — including that the observer cell
stays empty rather than inferred."""@en .
